pending_controller_recovery_fixes ["P1 \u2014 Before first baseline: NEEDS_REPAIR has no engineering-repair route. tools/r6_controller.py:253-274 sends it to PROFILE, although tools/r6_decision.py:100-105 explicitly returns REPAIR_AND_REVIEW. PROFILE only produces a profile or requests source preparation; tools/r6_source_preparation.py:90-92,152,172-174 forbids control-tool changes. Thus an analyzer/controller defect cannot be repaired by this execution chain, and unresolved evidence can proceed toward another profile without an explicit repair disposition. Add a bounded repair phase keyed to the failed job/decision, distinguishing offline evidence repair from a necessary new live attempt. Require repaired evidence and fresh exact review before dispatch; retain the original decision and native failure.", "P1 \u2014 Before first baseline: failed/rejected events are preserved but permanently selected again. tools/r6_start_baseline.py:37-54 reuses the same initial review; tools/r6_initial_baseline.py:117-120 rejects MUST_FIX without a replacement route. tools/r6_finalize_job.py:47-61,77-102 selects exactly one analysis/decision per job; tools/r6_cpu_analysis.py:72-82,116-119,184-190 rejects additional analysis events or changed sources and rethrows failed receipts. tools/r6_batch.py:29-34,92-93 rethrows recorded model failure; source preparation similarly binds one event per parent and rejects PREPARATION_INCOMPLETE at tools/r6_source_preparation.py:57-59,163. Controller exceptions preserve the same phase and exit at tools/r6_controller.py:382-392. Add explicitly linked, newly charged repair attempts after verified cleanup and side-effect reconciliation, with an active-attempt selector and immutable predecessors; never reset a claim or reinterpret missing receipts as permission to retry. The revision chain at tools/r6_controller_revision.py:74-82,159-169 correctly preserves cursor/request/receipts, but does not supersede these phase bindings. Preserve its fail-closed behavior and add phase-specific transitions rather than disabling pin checks.", "P1 \u2014 Before deployment, establish the concrete no-model-wait handoff and evidence source; downstream audit ingestion also needs repair. tools/r6_delivery_evidence.py:55-71 unconditionally reports platform_inactivity_proven=false and pure_wait_model_calls=UNKNOWN. tools/r6_final_audit_packet.py:93-107 copies that result into the final packet, and tools/r6_final_independent_review.py:145-147,160-163 consequently makes final PASS unreachable even if genuine external inactivity evidence later exists. Local wait records cover registered workers only; they cannot prove platform inactivity. Add a validated, pinned external handoff/wake-history evidence input covering actual wait windows, retaining UNKNOWN when unavailable. Capture it from the first deployment, verify detached identity/lock/logs with bounded checks, and exit the launch session. Final formatting can follow later; evidence capture cannot be reconstructed by declaring unknown intervals zero. No platform activity or inactivity was proven in this audit.", "P2 \u2014 Downstream, event-triggered before the affected phase resumes: confirmation/final repair remains a terminal boundary. tools/r6_controller.py:258-267 raises on a confirmation NEEDS_REPAIR; :213-235 parks final MUST_FIX permanently. tools/r6_final_sequence.py:24-29,43-64 binds one immutable batch, while tools/r6_holdout_publication.py:82-94 accepts only the original freeze/review. These guards prevent unsafe reuse but provide no authorized repair continuation. Add narrowly scoped replacement attempts/reviews when such failures occur, preserving completed seed results, failed receipts and budgets. Changed confirmation inputs must receive fresh acceptance. After holdout exposure, independently classify implementation/scoring defects, retain and mark contaminated data as development, and use a new unseen holdout only if the preregistered protocol and remaining budget permit; otherwise deliver an explicitly limited/incomplete result. Do not reopen tuning under the old holdout or inherit its approval. This work need not delay the first baseline once the basic repair handoff above exists."]
pending_controller_recovery_followup_fixes ["P1 \u2014 Before autonomous baseline deployment: connect baseline failures to a bounded repair handoff. tools/r6_controller.py:269-278 invokes FIRST_JOB, but :416-426 only saves INCOMPLETE_EXCEPTION and raises. tools/r6_start_baseline.py:37-53, tools/r6_finalize_job.py:47-61,77-103 and tools/r6_source_preparation.py:296-321 consume existing active attempts; they do not create a repaired successor when the selected attempt fails. The replacement primitives are real, but their existence does not complete autonomous recovery. Add a durable event keyed to the exact failed phase/receipt and a bounded authorized repair disposition that invokes the existing replacement registration after cleanup and evidence reconciliation. STOP, ambiguous ownership and missing receipts must remain fail-closed. This needs only the initial-review/closed-baseline-analysis/decision boundary, not every future failure route or a generic scheduling framework.", "P1 \u2014 Before first baseline evidence capture: distinguish observed waiter segments from complete job-wait coverage. tools/r6_submit.py:175-191 submits before tools/r6_audited_job_handoff.py:33 starts its audit; tools/r6_wait_audit.py:21-38 starts at the current observation time. A crash after submission but before that first record can therefore restart with a fresh, apparently complete audit and omit earlier live waiting. tools/r6_platform_wait.py:116-134 checks supplied segments but never compares their union with authoritative job start/closure boundaries or validates coverage between segments; tools/r6_delivery_evidence.py:63-76 can promote them to a zero-call result. An in-memory counterexample with windows [100,110] and [190,200] and a root turn [120,180] returns platform_inactivity_proven=true; this demonstrates the validator limitation, not a production occurrence. Persist the required coverage start before submission effects, bind it to exact job/process lifecycle evidence, and require complete interval coverage or explicitly retain UNKNOWN. Also bind trace completeness to a supported capture/flush or writer-health boundary: reading a stale, newline-complete log at a later wall-clock time is not by itself proof of coverage through that time. Preserve existing prefix, ownership, late-append and restart-gap rejection checks.", "P1 \u2014 Deployment/platform prerequisite: establish a real detached handoff before new dispatch. tools/r6_platform_wait.py:103-113 intentionally rejects an open root turn; tools/r6_run_job.py:56-57 and tools/r6_submit.py:104-105,124 invoke it. These checks observe platform activity and cannot disable automatic Goal continuation. tools/r6_controller_revision.py:192-198 starts a fresh child but does not establish detached service ownership or a platform handoff. Arrange the supported platform transition away from automatic inference, start the single local controller through existing tmux/service facilities, record PID/starttime/boot identity, lock, logs and recovery command, and perform bounded handoff checks before ending the launch turn. Have a bounded local gate handle the handoff transition rather than losing the controller to the expected open-turn exception. Do not bypass preflight or claim zero-model production waiting while automatic continuation remains active. Recovered sandbox access and already provisioned first-baseline volumes are not current administrator blockers.", "P2 \u2014 Downstream, when an analysis replacement is needed: honor its validated timeout during ordinary controller reentry. tools/r6_analysis_repair.py:75-107 permits a changed timeout as a legitimate repaired input, but tools/r6_finalize_job.py:38,50-58 compares the active replacement against the caller's default timeout_seconds=3600; finish_and_decide at :115 supplies no override. A valid replacement with a different timeout therefore fails FINALIZE_ANALYSIS_BINDING instead of resuming. Select the validated active request's bounded execution settings, or propagate its exact repaired settings through the controller. Test registration followed by the normal finalizer/controller entry, not only direct helper handoff. This does not block a fresh baseline using the default analysis settings.", "P2 \u2014 Downstream, event-triggered before the affected phase resumes: offline/control-code and frozen-phase repair remain unfinished. tools/r6_source_preparation.py:150-154 explicitly requires PREPARATION_INCOMPLETE for offline-only or controller changes, and :204 rejects that result; tools/r6_source_repair.py:23-24 restricts writable scope. The accepted-revision mechanism at tools/r6_controller_revision.py:99-173 and tools/r6_controller_resume.py:22-82 reviews/resumes an already supplied repair, but does not produce one. tools/r6_controller.py:241-246 parks frozen-phase repair and :220-221,266-268 parks final MUST_FIX. Preserve those protections and add only the concrete repair writer/disposition required by actual evidence, followed by exact review and fresh-process reentry where needed. For a crash after a resume claim, use ordinary exact-job recovery under the same writer lock; never clear or replay that claim. Confirmation and post-holdout repair need separate accepted inputs and contamination handling, not inherited approval. These phase-specific implementations can be deferred beyond the first baseline once the bounded failure handoff exists."]
